### Issue 4: Duplicate Static Field Initialization in Constructor

Every Animal subclass initializes `foodSources` and `killable` static fields in the constructor:

```java
// BEAR
public Bear(boolean randomAge, Field field, Location location)
{
    super(randomAge, field, location);
    foodSources = new HashSet<>();
    foodSources.add(Monkey.class);
    killable = new HashSet<>();
    killable.add(Monkey.class);
    killable.add(Plant.class);
}

// LEOPARD
public Leopard(boolean randomAge, Field field, Location location)
{
    super(randomAge, field, location);
    foodSources = new HashSet<>();
    foodSources.add(Sloth.class);
    killable = new HashSet<>();
    killable.add(Sloth.class);
    killable.add(Plant.class);
}
```

**Problems:**
1. Static fields should be initialized in static blocks, not instance constructors
2. Recreating HashSets on every instantiation is inefficient
3. No null-safety checks; if initialized multiple times, it replaces the previous set

**Fix:** Use static initializers:

```java
public class Bear extends Animal implements Infectable {
    private static final HashSet<Class> foodSources;
    private static final HashSet<Class> killable;

    static {
        foodSources = new HashSet<>();
        foodSources.add(Monkey.class);
        killable = new HashSet<>();
        killable.add(Monkey.class);
        killable.add(Plant.class);
    }
}
```

